---
import CodeBlock from "@/components/code-block.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/docs.astro";
import { docHref } from "@/lib/docs";

const loose = `object = "<type> <size>\\0" + content        type: blob | tree | commit | tag
id     = SHA-1(object)                        40 hex digits
stored = zlib(object) at .git/objects/<id[0..2]>/<id[2..]>

"hello\\n" as a blob:  "blob 6\\0hello\\n"  →  ce013625030ba8dba906f756967f9e9ca394464a
                     (the id \`printf 'hello\\n' | git hash-object --stdin\` prints)`;

const gitDir = `.git/
  HEAD                  ref: refs/heads/main      (a symbolic ref: the current branch)
  config                repository settings
  index                 the staging area, in git's binary index format
  objects/ce/0136…      loose objects, zlib-compressed
  objects/pack/*.pack   pack files, each with its .idx
  refs/heads/main       a branch: one object id
  refs/tags/v1.0        a tag
  packed-refs           many refs in one file (git pack-refs)
  logs/HEAD, logs/refs/ reflogs: where each ref pointed, and when
  MERGE_HEAD, rebase-merge/, sequencer/ …   the state of an operation in progress`;

const fetchDialogue = `client                                   server (git-upload-pack)
                                  ←      ref advertisement: <id> refs/heads/main …  (v0/v1)
                                         or capabilities, then ls-refs on request     (v2)
want <id>                          →
have <id> … (commits it already has)  →
                                  ←      ACK <id> / NAK  (common commits found)
done                               →
                                  ←      a pack of the objects the client lacks`;
---

<Docs title="How Git works here" description="The concepts behind StateWalker VCS and its live examples: Git's object model, loose objects and packs, refs and branches, the working copy, merging, the .git directory, and the wire protocol.">
  <h1>How Git works here</h1>
  <p>
    StateWalker VCS does not imitate git: it stores and exchanges the same bytes. This page
    explains the ideas the <a href="/examples">live examples</a> show, section by section, and
    points to the packages that implement each one. For how the packages are layered, read the
    <a href="/docs/architecture">architecture</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="objects">The object model</h2>
  <p>
    A repository's history is a set of immutable <strong>objects</strong>, each named by the hash
    of its content. There are four kinds:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>a <strong>blob</strong> is a file's bytes, with no name or mode;</li>
    <li>a <strong>tree</strong> is a directory: entries of mode, name and the id of a blob or another tree;</li>
    <li>a <strong>commit</strong> names a tree (the whole snapshot), its parent commits, an author, a committer and a message;</li>
    <li>an annotated <strong>tag</strong> names an object and adds a tagger and a message.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because an id is a hash of the content, the same file always has the same id, a snapshot
    shares every unchanged blob and tree with its parent, and any change to an object gives it a
    different id. <code>vcs-core</code> holds these objects behind typed stores
    (<code>history.blobs</code>, <code>trees</code>, <code>commits</code>, <code>tags</code>); see
    the <a href={docHref("packages/vcs-core/README.md")}>vcs-core reference</a>.
  </p>
  <p>Shown by: <a href="/examples/object-model">Object model</a> (a blob, a tree and a commit, built and checked).</p>

  <h2 id="loose-objects">Loose objects and ids</h2>
  <p>
    git stores an object on its own as a <strong>loose object</strong>: a short header, the type
    and the size, then the content; the id is the SHA-1 of those bytes, and the file holds them
    zlib-compressed. <code>vcs-core</code>'s object kernel writes exactly that form, whatever the
    storage behind it, and never re-encodes what git produced, so every id stays git's.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={loose} lang="text" title="a loose object" />
  <p>
    Only SHA-1 is implemented: SHA-256 repositories are not supported. Reading checks the header,
    the size and the zlib stream, and a received object is refused unless its bytes hash to its id.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shown by: <a href="/examples/object-model">Object model</a> (the browser's own zlib and SHA-1
    confirm each id) and <a href="/examples/git-directory">A real .git directory</a> (the files
    under <code>objects/</code>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="packs">Pack files</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>pack</strong> holds many objects in one file, with an <code>.idx</code> index to find
    each by id. Inside, an object may be stored as a <strong>delta</strong> against another one
    (by offset in the same pack, or by id), which is how git keeps history small. Packs are also
    what the protocol sends: a fetch or a push transfers one pack of the objects the other side
    lacks. <code>vcs-core</code> lists the objects to send (<code>listObjects</code>), writes
    version 2 packs (<code>writePack</code>, with a delta search), reads and indexes received ones;
    <code>vcs-store-files</code> adds <code>repack</code> and <code>gc</code> over a
    <code>.git</code> directory.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shown by: <a href="/examples/fetch-push">Fetch &amp; push</a> (each direction transfers a pack).
    More in the <a href={docHref("packages/vcs-core/README.md")}>vcs-core</a> and
    <a href={docHref("packages/vcs-store-files/README.md")}>vcs-store-files</a> references.
  </p>

  <h2 id="refs">Refs, HEAD and branches</h2>
  <p>
    Objects are immutable; what moves is <strong>refs</strong>, names that point at an object id.
    A <strong>branch</strong> is a ref under <code>refs/heads/</code>, a tag a ref under
    <code>refs/tags/</code>, and a <strong>remote-tracking</strong> ref under
    <code>refs/remotes/&lt;remote&gt;/</code> records where another repository's branch was at the
    last fetch. <strong>HEAD</strong> is usually a <em>symbolic</em> ref, naming the current branch
    rather than a commit: committing moves the branch HEAD names, and checking out a branch points
    HEAD at it. A <strong>refspec</strong> such as <code>+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/alice/*</code>
    says which remote refs a fetch writes to which local ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every ref store checks names as git does and refuses a value that is not a full object id;
    several refs can be updated in one atomic batch. <code>vcs-store-files</code> keeps refs as
    git does: one file per loose ref, <code>packed-refs</code>, and reflogs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shown by: <a href="/examples/quick-start">Quick start</a> (HEAD set to an unborn
    <code>main</code>), <a href="/examples/branching-merging">Branching &amp; merging</a> (create,
    switch) and <a href="/examples/fetch-push">Fetch &amp; push</a> (remote-tracking refs written
    by a refspec).
  </p>

  <h2 id="working-copy">History, checkout and worktree</h2>
  <p>
    The objects and refs are the <strong>history</strong>. What you edit is the
    <strong>worktree</strong>, the files. Between them sits the <strong>index</strong> (the staging
    area): the snapshot the next commit will record. <code>git add</code> stores a file's content
    as a blob and puts it in the index; <code>git commit</code> writes trees from the index, stores
    a commit of them and moves the current branch; <code>git status</code> compares HEAD, the index
    and the worktree.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here these are separate parts. <code>vcs-core</code> holds the history;
    <code>vcs-working-tree</code> holds the <strong>checkout</strong> (the index, HEAD and the
    state of a merge or rebase in progress) and the worktree; the three together are a
    <code>WorkingCopy</code>, and <code>Git.fromWorkingCopy</code> from <code>vcs-commands</code>
    runs commands on it. Each part has an in-memory and a file implementation
    (<a href="/docs/architecture#working-copy">architecture</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    Shown by: <a href="/examples/quick-start">Quick start</a> and the
    <a href="/examples/playground">playground</a>, whose panels show the worktree, the status and
    the log.
  </p>

  <h2 id="merge">Merging</h2>
  <p>
    <code>git merge</code> joins another branch's history into the current one. It first finds the
    <strong>merge base</strong>, the best common ancestor of the two commits.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      If the current commit is that ancestor, nothing needs combining: the merge
      <strong>fast-forwards</strong> and the branch simply moves to the other commit (the default;
      <code>FastForwardMode.NO_FF</code> always makes a merge commit, <code>FF_ONLY</code> refuses
      anything else).
    </li>
    <li>
      Otherwise it is a <strong>three-way merge</strong>: each path is compared in the base, ours
      and theirs. A change on one side only is taken; the same change on both sides is taken once;
      different changes to the same lines are a <strong>conflict</strong>. Without conflicts, a
      merge commit with two parents records the result.
    </li>
    <li>
      With conflicts, the merge stops (status <code>CONFLICTING</code>): the files hold conflict
      markers and the index keeps the three versions until you resolve them and commit.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The strategy is <code>MergeStrategy.RECURSIVE</code> by default, which handles several merge
    bases (criss-cross histories); <code>RESOLVE</code> uses a single base, and <code>OURS</code> /
    <code>THEIRS</code> keep one side's tree. <code>ContentMergeStrategy</code> (ours, theirs,
    union) settles conflicting hunks automatically. See the
    <a href={docHref("packages/vcs-commands/README.md")}>vcs-commands reference</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shown by: <a href="/examples/branching-merging">Branching &amp; merging</a> (a three-way merge
    commit) and the <a href="/examples/playground">playground</a> (try a conflict).
  </p>

  <h2 id="git-directory">The .git directory</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@statewalker/vcs-store-files</code> stores a repository in git's own layout, so
    <code>git log</code>, <code>git status</code> and <code>git fsck</code> work on it, and it reads
    what native git writes. All of its I/O goes through a <code>FilesApi</code>: the Node
    filesystem, memory, OPFS or any other implementation.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={gitDir} lang="text" title="what vcs-store-files reads and writes" />
  <p>
    The other backends (memory, SQL, key-value) keep the same objects and refs elsewhere, behind the
    same <code>History</code> interface (<a href="/docs/architecture#storage">architecture: storage</a>).
  </p>
  <p>
    Shown by: <a href="/examples/git-directory">A real .git directory</a> (every file written,
    then the folder as a zip for native git).
  </p>

  <h2 id="transport">The wire protocol</h2>
  <p>
    Two repositories exchange history with git's protocol. A fetch talks to the other side's
    <strong>upload-pack</strong> service, a push to its <strong>receive-pack</strong> service.
    Messages are <strong>pkt-lines</strong> (a 4-digit hex length, then the payload); with the
    side-band capability, the pack and the progress messages travel on separate bands of one
    stream.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Protocol v0/v1</strong>: the server opens with a ref advertisement (every ref and its
      id, plus capabilities such as <code>multi_ack_detailed</code>, <code>side-band-64k</code>,
      <code>ofs-delta</code>, <code>thin-pack</code>, <code>shallow</code>,
      <code>include-tag</code>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Protocol v2</strong>: the server advertises capabilities, and the client sends
      commands: <code>ls-refs</code> (list refs, optionally by prefix), <code>fetch</code> and
      <code>object-info</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Push</strong>: the client sends one command per ref (old id, new id, name) and a pack;
      the server checks and applies them and answers with a report-status, per ref.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>@statewalker/vcs-transport</code> implements both sides as state machines over a
    <code>Duplex</code>, so the same code runs over smart HTTP (<code>fetch</code>,
    <code>push</code>, <code>lsRemote</code>) and over any byte stream
    (<code>fetchOverDuplex</code>, <code>pushOverDuplex</code>, <code>serveOverDuplex</code>; a
    <code>MessagePort</code> in the example). See the
    <a href={docHref("packages/vcs-transport/README.md")}>vcs-transport reference</a> and
    <a href="/docs/architecture#transport">architecture: transport</a>.
  </p>

  <h3 id="negotiation">Fetch negotiation</h3>
  <p>
    The client says which commits it <em>wants</em> and which it already <em>has</em>; the server
    acknowledges the common ones, and then sends a pack with only the objects reachable from the
    wants and not from the common commits. A repository that already holds most of the history
    receives only what is new.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={fetchDialogue} lang="text" title="a fetch, simplified" />
  <p>
    Shown by: <a href="/examples/fetch-push">Fetch &amp; push</a> (a fetch over upload-pack, then a
    push over receive-pack, between two repositories in this tab).
  </p>

